---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Header from '../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
---

<Base
  title="The Antichrist and the Prophet — Dale Joseph"
  description="A political and spiritual novel about power, prophecy, and the battle for the soul of a nation. Coming 2026 from Dale Joseph."
>
  <Header slot="header" />

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="section book-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="label gold">A Novel</p>
      <h1>The Antichrist<br />and the Prophet</h1>
      <p class="byline">by Dale Joseph</p>
      <span class="status-badge">Coming 2026</span>
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <p class="logline">
        A political and spiritual novel about power, prophecy, and the battle for the soul of a nation.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- PREMISE -->
  <section class="section premise">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="premise-body">
        <p>
          Every generation produces its false prophets. Ours has produced something more dangerous:
          a man who doesn't believe he's false.
        </p>
        <p>
          Senator Marcus Cain has heard a voice his whole life—a whisper behind the noise of
          ambition, telling him that he is chosen, that the nation needs him, that history bends
          toward men like him. He has built a political career on the vocabulary of faith without
          the inconvenience of its demands. He speaks of God the way a contractor speaks of
          blueprints—useful framing for what he was going to build anyway. His followers hear
          conviction. His opponents see cynicism. Both are wrong.
        </p>
        <p>
          Elias Drum is a pastor in a dying mill town in eastern Ohio who has spent twenty years
          trying not to be noticed. He gave up on the national stage after a single season inside
          it nearly destroyed him. He tends his congregation, buries its dead, prays for its sick,
          and has made peace with smallness. Then one Sunday, a woman in the third pew hands him
          a printed transcript of Cain's speech at the National Prayer Breakfast, and something
          shifts behind Elias's eyes that his wife has not seen since before the Washington years.
        </p>
        <p>
          He recognizes what he's reading. Not politically. Spiritually. He has seen this architecture before—in the text, in history, in the mirror during the years he is not proud of.
          The question isn't whether Marcus Cain is a bad man.
          The question is what it costs to say so out loud. And whether silence is its own
          kind of collaboration.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- CENTRAL TENSION -->
  <section class="section section--dark tension">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <h2>Two Men. One Country. An Ancient Question.</h2>
      <p class="tension-intro">
        The novel moves between their worlds—the senate floor and the sanctuary, the television
        studio and the hospital waiting room—as each man's private convictions collide with
        public consequence.
      </p>
      <div class="figures">
        <div class="figure">
          <p class="figure-label gold">Marcus Cain</p>
          <p class="figure-role">The Senator</p>
          <p>
            Brilliant. Charismatic. Genuinely convinced of his own righteousness. Cain doesn't
            perform belief—he has constructed a theology in which his ambitions and God's will
            are indistinguishable. He has built a coalition of the afraid, the faithful, and the
            furious. He is not what his enemies think he is. He is something harder to fight.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="figure">
          <p class="figure-label gold">Elias Drum</p>
          <p class="figure-role">The Pastor</p>
          <p>
            Reluctant. Flawed. A man with his own history of self-deception who knows exactly
            how easy it is to mistake a hunger for power for a call from God. He has no platform,
            no allies, no plan. He has a congregation of two hundred, a voice that carries, and
            a theological education he has spent a decade trying to forget. He is the wrong man.
            He may be the only man.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- WHAT THE BOOK IS ABOUT -->
  <section class="section about-book">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <h2>What This Novel Is Really About</h2>
      <div class="about-grid">
        <div class="about-item">
          <h3 class="gold">Discernment</h3>
          <p>
            How do you tell the genuine from the counterfeit when both speak the same language,
            cite the same scripture, and claim the same God? The novel refuses easy answers.
            Both men are partly right. Both are capable of terrible self-deception. The question
            of discernment is not asked once—it runs under every scene.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="about-item">
          <h3 class="gold">Complicity</h3>
          <p>
            Every institution in this book—the church, the media, the party, the family—faces
            the same question: what is the cost of speaking, and what is the cost of silence?
            The novel is interested in how good people become accomplices to things they privately
            oppose, and what it actually takes to stop.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="about-item">
          <h3 class="gold">The Soul of a Nation</h3>
          <p>
            The battle isn't between faith and politics. It's within each. The novel is set in a
            country that can still go either way—not a dystopia, not a paradise, but a place
            under genuine pressure where the outcome is not guaranteed. The America in this book
            is recognizable because it is real.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <blockquote>
        "The most dangerous lie is the one you've told yourself so many times it no longer
        feels like a lie. It just feels like your calling."
      </blockquote>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- AUTHOR NOTE -->
  <section class="section section--dark author-note">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <h2>A Note on Why This Book</h2>
      <p>
        Dale Joseph spent three decades in hospital infrastructure—systems where the gap between
        what people say and what they mean can cost lives. He learned to read the space between
        the words. To watch what systems actually do rather than what they claim to do.
      </p>
      <p>
        He brought that same attention to the intersection of faith and power in American public
        life, and what he saw troubled him enough to write about it at length in nonfiction.
        But some truths are too particular for argument. Some things can only be said in a story.
      </p>
      <p>
        <em>The Antichrist and the Prophet</em> is not an allegory and not a polemic. It is an
        attempt to honestly imagine two men—one who has weaponized faith, one who is trying to
        reclaim it—and let them speak for themselves long enough that the reader is not entirely
        sure, for at least a few chapters, which one is which.
      </p>
      <a href="/about" class="btn">About Dale Joseph</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- EMAIL SIGNUP -->
  <section class="section email-capture" id="notify">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="divider"></div>
      <h2>Be First to Know</h2>
      <p>
        When <em>The Antichrist and the Prophet</em> is ready, you'll hear it here first—along
        with excerpts, essays on its themes, and the story of how it got written.
        No noise. No selling. Just the work.
      </p>
      <form
        action="https://formspree.io/f/maqpvlgz"
        method="POST"
        class="email-form"
      >
        <input type="hidden" name="book" value="The Antichrist and the Prophet" />
        <div class="form-row">
          <input
            type="email"
            name="email"
            placeholder="your@email.com"
            required
            aria-label="Email address"
          />
          <button type="submit" class="btn btn--filled">Notify Me at Launch</button>
        </div>
        <p class="form-note muted">
          Your information is never shared. Unsubscribe at any time.
        </p>
      </form>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Footer slot="footer" />
</Base>

<style>
  .label {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.14em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .book-hero {
    padding-bottom: 3rem;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .book-hero h1 {
    font-size: clamp(2.4rem, 6vw, 3.6rem);
    line-height: 1.1;
    margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
  }

  .byline {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
  }

  .status-badge {
    display: inline-block;
    font-size: 0.75rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    border: 1px solid var(--gold);
    color: var(--gold);
    padding: 0.3rem 0.85rem;
  }

  .logline {
    font-size: 1.2rem;
    color: var(--gold-light);
    font-style: italic;
    line-height: 1.6;
    max-width: 580px;
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  /* PREMISE */
  .premise-body p {
    font-size: 1.05rem;
    line-height: 1.85;
  }

  .premise-body p:first-child {
    font-size: 1.25rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    color: var(--text);
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  /* TENSION */
  .section--dark {
    background: var(--bg-elevated);
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
  }

  .tension h2 {
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .tension-intro {
    color: var(--text-muted);
    font-style: italic;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
  }

  .figures {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(260px, 1fr));
    gap: 2.5rem;
  }

  .figure-label {
    font-size: 0.8rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.12em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 0.2rem;
  }

  .figure-role {
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    margin-bottom: 0.85rem;
    color: var(--text);
  }

  .figure p:last-child {
    font-size: 0.97rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  /* WHAT THE BOOK IS ABOUT */
  .about-book h2 {
    margin-bottom: 2rem;
  }

  .about-grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(220px, 1fr));
    gap: 2.5rem;
    margin-bottom: 2.5rem;
  }

  .about-item h3 {
    font-size: 1rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.03em;
    margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
  }

  .about-item p {
    font-size: 0.97rem;
    color: var(--text-muted);
    line-height: 1.75;
  }

  .about-book blockquote {
    border-left: 3px solid var(--gold);
    padding: 0.75rem 1.5rem;
    color: var(--gold-light);
    font-style: italic;
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    line-height: 1.65;
    margin: 0;
  }

  /* AUTHOR NOTE */
  .author-note h2 {
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
  }

  .author-note p {
    font-size: 1.02rem;
    line-height: 1.8;
  }

  .author-note .btn {
    margin-top: 1rem;
  }

  /* EMAIL */
  .email-capture h2 {
    margin-bottom: 1rem;
  }

  .email-form {
    margin-top: 1.75rem;
  }

  .form-row {
    display: flex;
    gap: 0.75rem;
    max-width: 520px;
  }

  .form-row input {
    flex: 1;
  }

  .form-note {
    font-size: 0.85rem;
    margin-top: 0.75rem;
  }

  @media (max-width: 520px) {
    .form-row {
      flex-direction: column;
    }

    .figures,
    .about-grid {
      grid-template-columns: 1fr;
    }
  }
</style>
